# Heki — a concrete async PERSISTENCE adapter. The contract : it DECLARES the
# family it implements (the inverted arrow) ; the family never names it. Always
# async, never locks.
#
# The bind `Pizzas::Order.persisted_by("Heki")` resolves IFF Heki's declared
# port (persistence) carries the `persisted_by` verb — the typed attach
# checkpoint. An adapter whose port lacks the verb fails the attach, loudly,
# at wiring. Per-deployment values (the store `dir`) live in .world ; this
# declaration only NAMES the field, matching hecksagain's own vendored
# heki.adapter exactly.
#
# `field :dir` added (parser-removal plan, Phase 1a) : this file's own
# `family "persistence"` never actually parsed before this pass (`.family`/
# `Hecks.adapter`'s old `family` call both crashed boot), so this corpus copy
# never successfully registered — the vendored library's OWN "Heki" adapter
# (which DOES declare `field :dir`) was silently the only one ever live.
# Fixing the crash let THIS copy load for the first time, and
# Registry#add_adapter is a flat name-keyed overwrite (load_library runs
# before load_domain in the same boot) — so this copy now shadows the
# library's, and needed the field it was always missing. Found live : every
# .world file setting `dir:` on Heki failed with "Heki does not declare :dir"
# across 26 domain roots the moment this file started loading successfully.
Hecks.adapter "Heki" do
  port  "persistence"
  field :dir
end
